"use strict";(self.webpackChunkspa_template=self.webpackChunkspa_template||[]).push([[450],{4450:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"created":"2015-09-06T15:42:00","modified":"2015-09-06T15:42:00","id":1023,"title":"PInvoke, be careful with your returned data types...","article":"<p>Just a cautionary note if your new to using pinvoke (Platform Invoke) from .Net code, you need to remember that you connecting two things that are not the same and have different general behaviours. If you remember that it can be an exceptional powerful tool to fill any gaps in the .Net framework.</p>\\n<p>An example of this is returning the correct data types, many functions you can instantiate return a <code>DWORD</code> but many people mistake this as an <code>int</code> rather than an <code>unsigned int</code>, resulting in some unexpected behaviour.</p>\\n<p>To explain this below is an example showing using <code>GetFileAttributes</code> from <code>kernel32.dll</code></p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-cs\\">[<span class=\\"hljs-meta\\">DllImport(<span class=\\"hljs-meta-string\\">\\"kernel32.dll\\"</span>, CharSet = CharSet.Unicode, SetLastError = true)</span>]\\n<span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">internal</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">static</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">extern</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">uint</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">GetFileAttributes</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-params\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">string</span> lpFileName</span>)</span>;</code></pre>\\n<p>According to the Windows API it returns a <code>DWORD</code> which is the equivalent of an unsigned int, but we could mark it as an int without it throwing exception when run. </p>\\n<p>The problem comes from the unmanaged code returning an invalid handle, in this case the file could not be found, technically this is -1 but minus values are not allowed in unsigned numbers so it gets converted to 4294967295 whereas an int actually returns -1.</p>\\n<p>The reason a uint returns 4294967295 is because when you take away from an unsigned value and go below zero it cant go into minus figures as they don&#39;t exist so it &#39;rolls&#39; over to the maximum value of the unsigned type and continues taking away values. Rather than remembering the number 4294967295 .Net has the constant <code>uint.MaxValue</code> you can compare against and use.</p>\\n<p>Having a response that could potentially be two values but mean the same thing, makes checking values more difficult and can cause bugs, especially when other developers are expecting a particular value.</p>\\n<h2 id=\\"summary\\">Summary</h2>\\n<p>Try to stick with values that are explicitly declared by the API your calling, its worth the extra check as you will never know when it will come back to bite you.</p>\\n","showInResults":true,"tags":["c#","com"]}')}}]);